 ## Background
-The problem of aliasing in volume rendering and splatting has been addressed since the early days of object-order rendering. Early work by Westover established the theoretical basis for splatting and the importance of footprint size relative to pixel spacing to avoid aliasing [S7, book]. Subsequent research introduced hybrid approaches combining polygonal slicing for high-density regions with splatting for sparse regions to maintain visual continuity [S1, unofficial]. Temporal aliasing in animated volumes led to the development of motion-blurred splats, which extend the spatial footprint to cover the motion path within a frame [S1, unofficial]. More recently, with the rise of 3D Gaussian Splatting and surface splatting for inverse rendering, advanced filtering techniques such as MIP-filtering and center-precomputed covariance estimation have been adapted to handle high-frequency details in real-time rendering pipelines [S3, unofficial].
+Anti-aliasing techniques in splatting have evolved to address spatial and temporal undersampling artifacts. Spatial methods include line coverage calculations based on point-to-line distances scaled by viewport and field of view [S1, unofficial], and screen-space ratio filters for adaptive sampling density [S2, unofficial]. Modern inverse rendering pipelines utilize center-precomputed MIP filters to eliminate aliasing with minimal per-pixel overhead [S3, unofficial], while image-aligned splatting variants employ z-averaged kernels for smoother results [S4].
 
